  "worked_solution": "From the slope field, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $y=-1$ and $y=1$ are equilibrium solutions.\n\n- For all $y\\in (1,\\infty ),$ the slopes of the slope field are positive. Therefore, $f(y)>0$ in this region.\n\n- For all $y\\in (-1,1),$ the slopes of the slope field are negative. Therefore, $f(y)<0$ in this region.\n\n- For all $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1),$ the slopes of the slope field are negative. Therefore, $f(y)<0$ in this region.\n\nNow, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis.\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions.\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0.$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0.$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/62b3d23025e8b2716dd584678f1aef2ba3132a66250d0999cb13a2281347ee89.png)",
